# Production deploy

Runbook: `docs/deployment/VPS.md`. Read it before changing anything here.
Server access, keys, controls, and the change log: `docs/deployment/SECURITY.md`.
Any change on the VPS or in GitHub settings follows its rules and gets a
change-log line.

## How releases happen

- A push to `main` runs CI; when CI succeeds, `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`
  runs `scripts/deploy-vps.sh` on the exact commit CI tested.
- The script builds a new release in `/var/www/webofficial/releases/<id>`,
  backs up both databases, runs `prisma migrate deploy`, tests a candidate on
  port 4001, then switches the `current` symlink and reloads PM2 `nextjs-app`.
- Deploying never happens from a dirty tree: the script exports the commit with
  `git archive`.

## Rules

- Deploy only when the user asks. Normally that means merging to `main`, not
  running the script by hand.
- Manual fallback, only when the workflow fails and the user agrees:
  `npm run deploy:vps -- --check` (read-only), then `npm run deploy:vps` from a
  clean worktree of CI-green `main`. Never run it while the workflow is running.
- Watch a deploy with `gh run list --workflow deploy.yml` and `gh run watch`.
- Never print, copy, or commit `/var/www/webofficial/.env.local` or any secret.
  SSH uses the `webofficial-vps` key alias; never store a password.
- Never touch shared VPS data: `public/uploads/`, `public/music/uploads/`,
  `backups/`, `theory/`.
- A destructive migration (drop or rename a column or table) needs a
  maintenance window and a restore plan agreed with the user first.
- Changing the workflow or deploy script: keep the job names and
  `workflow_run` trigger that `deploy.yml` relies on, and update
  `docs/deployment/VPS.md` in the same pull request.
